.EE
.PP
Run static analysis without execution, or format a script to standard
output[cite: 4].
Append \f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R] to write safe lint fixes or formatted code
directly to the file in place[cite: 4].
.IP
.EX
kosh \-\-lint script.kosh
kosh \-\-format script.kosh
kosh \-\-lint \-\-format \-\-apply script.kosh
.EE
.SH COMMAND\-LINE SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
kosh [options] [script [argument ...]]
kosh \-c command [argument ...]
kosh \-s [argument ...]
kosh \-\-lint [\-c command ...] [file ...]
kosh \-\-format [file]
kosh \-\-lint \-\-format [file]
kosh [\-\-lint] [\-\-format] \-\-apply file ...
kosh \-\-as\-language\-server
.EE
.SH KOSHKA ARCHITECTURE AND CORE FEATURES
.SS Execution moods and option invariants
Koshka partitions shell behavior into four moods[cite: 4].
In this manual, \f[CR]bash\f[R] and \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] are referred
to as the \f[B]Bash moods\f[R], \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R]
(where \f[CR]posix\f[R] is on) are the \f[B]POSIX moods\f[R], and
\f[CR]bash\f[R], \f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R], and \f[CR]sh\f[R] are the
\f[B]compatibility moods\f[R][cite: 4].
.PP
