.SH BASH AND POSIX LANGUAGE SPECIFICATION
.SS Invocation, operands, and compatibility startup
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Input source precedence\f[R] \- Evaluated in strict order:
\f[CR]\-s\f[R] (stdin), \f[CR]\-c\f[R] (inline command strings),
\f[CR]script\f[R] operand (where \f[CR]\-\f[R] selects stdin; a
directory fails with \f[CR]126\f[R], an unopenable file fails with
\f[CR]127\f[R]), \f[CR]\-i\f[R] or terminal stdin (interactive editor),
or non\-interactive stdin[cite: 4].
Selecting multiple sources emits a warning and uses the
highest\-priority source, except \f[CR]\-s \-i\f[R] on a terminal starts
the interactive editor[cite: 4].
Passing \f[CR]\-i\f[R] without a terminal fails after startup files
complete[cite: 4].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Positional parameter assignment (\f[CB]$0\f[B] and
\f[CB]$1..$N\f[B])\f[R] \- For scripts, \f[CR]$0\f[R] is the script path
and remaining operands become \f[CR]$1..$N\f[R][cite: 4].
For \f[CR]\-c\f[R], the first operand after the command string(s)
becomes \f[CR]$0\f[R] and subsequent operands become
\f[CR]$1..$N\f[R][cite: 4].
For \f[CR]\-s\f[R] and interactive sessions, \f[CR]$0\f[R] remains the
shell invocation name and all operands become \f[CR]$1..$N\f[R][cite:
4].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Invocation failure and rescue mode\f[R] \- When \f[CR]$0\f[R]
begins with \f[CR]\-\f[R] (login shell) and option parsing fails, the
shell warns and enters \f[B]rescue mode\f[R] (skipping all settings and
startup files)[cite: 4].
If only \f[CR]KOSH_FLAGS\f[R] fails to parse, \f[CR]KOSH_FLAGS\f[R] is
dropped[cite: 4].
If command\-line flags fail to parse in a login shell, only
\f[CR]\-r\f[R] is retained and commands are read from stdin[cite: 4].
All other invalid option combinations exit with status
\f[CR]2\f[R][cite: 4].
.PP
.TS
