.TE
.SS Pathname globbing and pattern matching
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Sort order and no\-match behavior\f[R] \- Glob results sort by byte
value in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R], and by \f[CR]LC_COLLATE\f[R]
order in Bash moods[cite: 4].
A pattern with no matches is a fatal error in \f[CR]kosh\f[R], stays
literal in compatibility moods by default, expands to nothing under
\f[CR]nullglob\f[R], and errors under \f[CR]failglob\f[R][cite: 4].
Assigning non\-empty \f[CR]GLOBIGNORE\f[R] filters matching results and
automatically enables \f[CR]dotglob\f[R][cite: 4].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Extended globs (\f[CB]extglob\f[B])\f[R] \- \f[CR]?()\f[R],
\f[CR]*()\f[R], \f[CR]+()\f[R], \f[CR]\[at]()\f[R], and \f[CR]!()\f[R]
are enabled by default in \f[CR]kosh\f[R], always enabled inside
\f[CR][[ == ]]\f[R] and \f[CR][[ != ]]\f[R], and controlled by
\f[CR]shopt \-s extglob\f[R] elsewhere[cite: 4].
.SS Redirections
Redirections process left\-to\-right; prefixing \f[CR]{var}\f[R]
allocates a descriptor \f[CR]>= 10\f[R] and stores the integer in
\f[CR]var\f[R][cite: 4].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Operators\f[R] \- \f[CR][n]<word\f[R], \f[CR][n]>word\f[R]
(respects \f[CR]noclobber\f[R]), \f[CR][n]>|word\f[R] (overrides
\f[CR]noclobber\f[R]), \f[CR][n]>>word\f[R] (inside a loop body,
\f[CR]>>\f[R] opens the file once for the entire loop lifetime),
\f[CR][n]<>word\f[R], \f[CR]&>word\f[R] / \f[CR]>&word\f[R] /
\f[CR]&>>word\f[R] (rejected in \f[CR]sh\f[R]), \f[CR][n]<&m\f[R] /
\f[CR][n]>&m\f[R], \f[CR][n]<&\-\f[R] / \f[CR][n]>&\-\f[R] (close),
\f[CR][n]<&m\-\f[R] / \f[CR][n]>&m\-\f[R] (move and close \f[CR]m\f[R]),
\f[CR][n]<<word\f[R] / \f[CR][n]<<\-word\f[R] (here\-document), and
\f[CR][n]<<<word\f[R] (here\-string, rejected in \f[CR]sh\f[R])[cite:
4].
\f[CR]/dev/tcp\f[R] is not emulated[cite: 4].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Expansion scope\f[R] \- In \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R], all
redirection targets expand in the parent shell[cite: 4].
In Bash moods, redirections on external programs, bare redirections, and
subshells expand in the child process[cite: 4].
.SS Arithmetic evaluation (\f[CR]$(( ))\f[R], \f[CR](( ))\f[R], \f[CR]let\f[R])
Supports standard C operator precedence plus \f[CR]**\f[R] and
\f[CR]**=\f[R] (exponentiation) and \f[CR]0b\f[R] binary literals
alongside decimal, \f[CR]0\f[R] octal, \f[CR]0x\f[R] hex, and
\f[CR]base#digits\f[R] (\f[CR]2\f[R]..\f[CR]64\f[R])[cite: 4].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Precision and unset variables\f[R] \- In \f[CR]kosh\f[R] (or under
\f[CR]\-\-enable\-extended\-arithmetic\f[R] /
\f[CR]interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers=on\f[R]), arithmetic uses
arbitrary\-precision integers and finite decimals
(\f[CR]$((2**100))\f[R] is exact; \f[CR]$((7.0/2))\f[R] is
\f[CR]3.5\f[R]), and expanding an unset variable is an error[cite: 4].
In compatibility moods by default, arithmetic uses wrapping 64\-bit
signed integers and evaluates unset variables as \f[CR]0\f[R][cite: 4].
.SS Conditional expressions (\f[CR][[ ]]\f[R], \f[CR]test\f[R], \f[CR][ ]\f[R])
Supports file primaries (\f[CR]\-a\f[R], \f[CR]\-e\f[R], \f[CR]\-b\f[R],
\f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-f\f[R], \f[CR]\-p\f[R],
\f[CR]\-S\f[R], \f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-L\f[R], \f[CR]\-g\f[R],
\f[CR]\-u\f[R], \f[CR]\-k\f[R], \f[CR]\-r\f[R], \f[CR]\-w\f[R],
\f[CR]\-x\f[R], \f[CR]\-O\f[R], \f[CR]\-G\f[R], \f[CR]\-s\f[R],
\f[CR]\-t\f[R], \f[CR]\-ef\f[R], \f[CR]\-nt\f[R], \f[CR]\-ot\f[R]),
string primaries (\f[CR]\-z\f[R], \f[CR]\-n\f[R], \f[CR]==\f[R],
\f[CR]=\f[R], \f[CR]!=\f[R], \f[CR]<\f[R], \f[CR]>\f[R]),
variable/option primaries (\f[CR]\-v\f[R] in \f[CR][[ ]]\f[R] only,
\f[CR]\-R\f[R] outside \f[CR]sh\f[R], \f[CR]\-o\f[R]), ERE matching
(\f[CR]=\[ti]\f[R] in \f[CR][[ ]]\f[R] only, populating
\f[CR]BASH_REMATCH\f[R] where \f[CR].\f[R] matches newlines and invalid
regexes return \f[CR]1\f[R]), and integer comparisons (\f[CR]\-eq\f[R],
\f[CR]\-ne\f[R], \f[CR]\-lt\f[R], \f[CR]\-le\f[R], \f[CR]\-gt\f[R],
\f[CR]\-ge\f[R])[cite: 4].
In \f[CR]kosh\f[R] and \f[CR]sh\f[R], \f[CR]test\f[R] / \f[CR][\f[R]
rejects \f[CR]==\f[R] with a hint to use \f[CR]=\f[R][cite: 4].
.SS Aliases, functions, and command execution
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Aliases\f[R] \- An alias replaces the first word of a simple
command with a string, expanded when the command runs, so an alias
defined earlier on the same line or in the same compound command applies
at once, and an alias in a function body takes its value at each call.
A replacement ending with a blank also checks the next word.
Chains are followed, a word already being replaced is not replaced
again, and quoted words, words with a backslash, words produced by an
expansion, and reserved words are never aliases.
Aliases expand while \f[CR]expand_aliases\f[R] is on (see Default option
states across moods), \f[CR]alias\f[R] and \f[CR]unalias\f[R] manage
them, and \f[CR]BASH_ALIASES\f[R] holds them.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Functions\f[R] \- A function runs in the current shell with its own
positional parameters, and \f[CR]$0\f[R] stays the script name.
Its status is the \f[CR]return\f[R] value or the status of the last
command.
A function called from a guarded and\-or operand or a negation keeps
\f[CR]errexit\f[R] suppressed throughout its body.
The \f[CR]RETURN\f[R] trap fires at the end of every function body in
the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, for bodies that \f[CR]functrace\f[R] follows
in the Bash moods, and is rejected in the \f[CR]sh\f[R] mood.
\f[CR]export \-f\f[R] exports a function as \f[CR]BASH_FUNC_name%%\f[R]
for Bash children, and Koshka does not import exported functions.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Command lookup\f[R] \- After expansion, a word holding a slash runs
that path directly.
Other words resolve in the order given under Command execution lifecycle
at the top of this manual, and running a command or \f[CR]hash\f[R]
caches its path until \f[CR]PATH\f[R] is assigned, \f[CR]hash \-r\f[R]
runs, or a directory change affects a relative \f[CR]PATH\f[R] entry.
A program runs in a separate process with the exported variables as its
environment.
A command that is not found returns \f[CR]127\f[R], a file that cannot
run returns \f[CR]126\f[R], and a command path ending in a directory
separator fails with \f[CR]126\f[R] when the target is not a directory.
A file without a recognized executable format runs as a shell script in
a new shell, without the variables, functions, and traps of the caller.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Directory commands\f[R] \- In an interactive shell, a single
directory path in command position changes to that directory as
\f[CR]cd\f[R] would, unless a function, builtin, program, or bundled
utility of that name exists.
A word of only dots moves up one directory per dot after the first.
\f[CR]autocd\f[R] controls this, is on in every mood, and never affects
scripts.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Execution environment\f[R] \- The shell environment consists of
open files, the working directory, the file creation mask, resource
limits, traps, variables and their attributes, functions, options,
aliases, the directory stack, and the job table.
A subshell, command substitution, separate pipeline stage, or
asynchronous list receives a copy whose changes do not reach the shell.
Caught traps reset to their defaults there, ignored traps stay ignored,
and a subshell cannot wait for a job of its parent.
A program receives the open descriptors, working directory, creation
mask, resource limits, ignored signals, and exported variables, and
every descriptor the shell opened for itself is closed in the program.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Environment variables\f[R] \- At startup the shell imports its
environment and marks every variable for export.
Inherited variables and variables marked with \f[CR]export\f[R] or
\f[CR]declare \-x\f[R] pass to every program, and a prefix assignment
adds a variable for one command.
Shell\-maintained values such as \f[CR]SECONDS\f[R], \f[CR]IFS\f[R],
\f[CR]SHELLOPTS\f[R], and the Koshka variables are not taken from the
environment.
On Windows, variable names are not case\-sensitive and a value holds at
most \f[CR]32,766\f[R] UTF\-16 units, so longer values reach a child
truncated.
Names beginning with \f[CR]KOSH_INTERNAL_\f[R] are reserved.
