.TE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Standard exit codes\f[R] \- \f[CR]0\f[R] (success), \f[CR]1\f[R]
(general/analysis failure), \f[CR]2\f[R] (syntax/usage error),
\f[CR]126\f[R] (found but non\-executable or directory), \f[CR]127\f[R]
(command/script not found), \f[CR]128 + n\f[R] (terminated by signal
\f[CR]n\f[R]: \f[CR]130\f[R] \f[CR]SIGINT\f[R], \f[CR]141\f[R]
\f[CR]SIGPIPE\f[R], \f[CR]143\f[R] \f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R])[cite: 4].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Signals and PID 1 reaper\f[R] \- The shell ignores
\f[CR]SIGPIPE\f[R] (resetting it to default in child processes) and
interactive shells additionally ignore \f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R],
\f[CR]SIGQUIT\f[R], \f[CR]SIGTSTP\f[R], and \f[CR]SIGTTIN\f[R][cite: 4].
When running as PID \f[CR]1\f[R], \f[CR]kosh\f[R] automatically reaps
adopted orphan processes and on \f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R] broadcasts
\f[CR]SIGTERM\f[R] + \f[CR]SIGCONT\f[R] to all processes, waits for
children, and exits with \f[CR]143\f[R][cite: 4].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Job control and history expansion\f[R] \- Background jobs are
addressed via \f[CR]%n\f[R], \f[CR]%+\f[R], \f[CR]%%\f[R], \f[CR]%\f[R],
\f[CR]%\-\f[R], \f[CR]%prefix\f[R], or \f[CR]%?substring\f[R] (requires
quoting in \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood to prevent globbing)[cite: 4].
The shell retains the 1024 most recent child exit statuses and never
sends \f[CR]SIGHUP\f[R] to background jobs on exit[cite: 4].
Interactive shells with \f[CR]histexpand=on\f[R] expand \f[CR]!\f[R]
history event designators (\f[CR]!!\f[R], \f[CR]!n\f[R],
\f[CR]!\-n\f[R], \f[CR]!str\f[R], \f[CR]!?str?\f[R], \f[CR]!#\f[R],
\f[CR]\[ha]old\[ha]new\[ha]\f[R]), word designators (\f[CR]:\[ha]\f[R],
\f[CR]:$\f[R], \f[CR]:*\f[R], \f[CR]:n\f[R], \f[CR]:n\-m\f[R]), and
modifiers (\f[CR]:h\f[R], \f[CR]:t\f[R], \f[CR]:r\f[R], \f[CR]:e\f[R],
\f[CR]:s/old/new/\f[R], \f[CR]:gs\f[R], \f[CR]:as\f[R], \f[CR]:Gs\f[R],
\f[CR]:&\f[R], \f[CR]:q\f[R], \f[CR]:x\f[R], \f[CR]:p\f[R]) before
parsing[cite: 4].
.SS Standard shell builtins reference
Special builtins are \f[CR]:\f[R], \f[CR].\f[R], \f[CR]break\f[R],
\f[CR]continue\f[R], \f[CR]eval\f[R], \f[CR]exec\f[R], \f[CR]exit\f[R],
\f[CR]export\f[R], \f[CR]readonly\f[R], \f[CR]return\f[R],
\f[CR]set\f[R], \f[CR]shift\f[R], \f[CR]times\f[R], \f[CR]trap\f[R],
\f[CR]unset\f[R], and (in Bash moods) \f[CR]source\f[R][cite: 4].
Every builtin accepts \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R] as its first argument (except
\f[CR]echo\f[R] and \f[CR]test\f[R] in compatibility moods, and
\f[CR][\f[R] in all moods)[cite: 4].
.PP
